Lex["1041"] = "bomos {bo'-mos}|AV - altar 1; 1|from the base of 939||noun masculine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> an elevated placeINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> very often a raised place on which to offer a sacrifice, an altar<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> βωμός b&#x14D;m&#xF3;s, bo&#39;-mos; from the base of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=939&page=1\">G939</a>; properly, a stand, i.e. (specially) an altar:&#8212;altar.";
Lex["1042"] = "gabbatha {gab-bath-ah'}|AV - Gabbatha 1; 1|of Aramaic origin cf 01355||noun proper location| INDENT0--Gabbatha = \"elevated or a platform\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a raised place, elevation<BR /><BR />INDENT0--<b>Additional information from the Outline of Biblical Usage:</b><br />In the Greek was called Lithostrotos, or the pavement of stones, as the Syrian version renders it: it is thought to be the room Gazith, in which the sanhedrin sat in the temple when they tried capital causes; and it was so called, because it was paved with smooth, square hewn stones: \"it was in the north part; half of it was holy, and half of it was common: and it had two doors, one for that part which was holy, and another for that part that was common; and in that half that was common the sanhedrin sat.\" So that into this part of it, and by this door, Pilate, though a Gentile, might enter. This place, in the language of the Jews, who at that time spoke Syrian, was Gabbatha, from its height, as it should seem; though the Syrian and Persian versions read Gaphiphtha, which signifies a fence or enclosure. Mention is made in the Talmud of the upper Gab in the mountain of the house; but whether the same with this Gabbatha, and whether this is the same with the chamber Gazith, is not certain. The Septuagint uses the same word as John here does, and calls by the same name the pavement of the temple on which Israelites fell and worshipped God. (2 Chr. 7:3) (Gill)<BR><BR>The Hebrew word for Pavement occurs only once in the Old Testament. In 2 Kings 16:17 we read, \"King Ahaz cut off the borders of the bases, and removed the laver from off them; and took down the sea from off the brazen oxen that were under it, and put it upon the pavement of stones.\" In Ahaz's case his act was the conclusive token of his abject apostasy. So here of Pilate coming down to the apostate Jews. In the former case it was a Jewish ruler dominated by a Gentile idolater; in the latter, a Gentile idolater dominated by Jews who had rejected the Messiah! (A.W. Pink, <i>Exposition of the Gospel of John</i>, p.1038)<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>γαββαθά gabbath&#xE1;, gab-bath-ah&#39;; of Chaldee origin (compare Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H1355\">H1355</a>); the knoll; gabbatha, a vernacular term for the Roman tribunal in Jerusalem:&#8212;Gabbatha.";
Lex["1043"] = "Gabriel {gab-ree-ale'}|AV - Gabriel 2; 2|of Hebrew origin 01403||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Gabriel = \"man of God\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> one of the angel princes or chiefs of the angels<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Γαβριήλ Gabri&#x1E17;l, gab-ree-ale&#39;; of Hebrew origin (Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H1403\">H1403</a>); Gabriel, an archangel:&#8212;Gabriel.";
Lex["1044"] = "gaggraina {gang'-grahee-nah}|AV - canker 1; 1|from graino (to gnaw)||noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a gangrene, a disease by which any part of the body suffering from inflammation becomes so corrupted that, unless a remedy be seasonably applied, the evil continually spreads, attacks other parts, and at last eats away the bones<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> γάγγραινα g&#xE1;ngraina, gang&#39;-grahee-nah; from &#x3B3;&#x3C1;&#x3B1;&#x1F77;&#x3BD;&#x3C9; gra&#xED;n&#x14D; (to gnaw); an ulcer ( \"gangrene\"):&#8212;canker.";
Lex["1045"] = "Gad {gad}|AV - Gad 1; 1|of Hebrew origin 01410||noun proper location| INDENT0--Gad = \"a troop\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> the seventh son of the patriarch Jacob, by Zilpah, Leah's maid<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Γάδ G&#xE1;d, gad; of Hebrew origin (Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H1410\">H1410</a>); Gad, a tribe of Israelites:&#8212;Gad.";
Lex["1046"] = "Gadarenos {gad-ar-ay-nos'}|AV - Gadarenes 3; 3|from Gadara (a town east of the Jordan)||adjective| INDENT0--Gadarenes = \"reward at the end\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> also called Gergesenes, was the capital of Peraea, situated opposite the south extremity of the Lake of Gennesaret to the south-east, but at some distance from the lake on the banks of the river Hieromax<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>Γαδαρηνός Gadar&#x113;n&#xF3;s, gad-ar-ay-nos&#39;; from &#x393;&#x3B1;&#x3B4;&#x3B1;&#x3C1;&#x1F71; Gadar&#xE1; (a town east of the Jordan); a Gadarene or inhabitant of Gadara:&#8212;Gadarene.";
Lex["1047"] = "gaza {gad'-zah}|AV - treasure 1; 1|of foreign origin||noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> the royal treasury, treasure, riches<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> γάζα g&#xE1;za, gad&#39;-zah; of foreign origin; a treasure:&#8212;treasure.";
Lex["1048"] = "Gaza {gad'-zah}|AV - Gaza 1; 1|of Hebrew origin 05804||noun proper location| INDENT0--Gaza = \"the fortified; the strong\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> one of the five chief cities of the Philistines in Palestine<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Γάζα G&#xE1;za, gad&#39;-zah; of Hebrew origin (Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H5804\">H5804</a>); Gazah (i.e. Azzah), a place in Palestine:&#8212;Gaza.";
Lex["1049"] = "gazophulakion {gad-zof-oo-lak'-ee-on}|AV - treasury 5; 5|from 1047 and 5438||noun neuter|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a repository of treasure, especially of public treasure, a treasury<BR /><BR />INDENT0--<b>Additional information from the Outline of Biblical Usage:</b><br />It is used to describe the apartments constructed in the courts of the temple, in which not only the sacred offerings and things needful for the service were kept, but in which the priests, etc, dwelt: Neh. 13:7; of the sacred treasury in which not only treasure but also public records were stored, and the property of widows and orphans was deposited. Josephus speaks of treasuries in the women's court of Herod's temple. In the N.T. near the treasury seems to used of that receptacle mentioned by the rabbis to which were fitted thirteen chests or boxes, i.e. trumpets, so called from their shape, and into which were put the contributions made voluntarily or paid yearly by the Jews for the service of the temple and the support of the poor.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> γαζοφυλάκιον gazophyl&#xE1;kion, gad-zof-oo-lak&#39;-ee-on; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1047&page=1\">G1047</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=5438&page=1\">G5438</a>; a treasure-house, i.e. a court in the temple for the collection-boxes:&#8212;treasury.";
Lex["1050"] = "Gaios {gah'-ee-os}|AV - Gaius (of Corinth) 2, Gaius (of Macedonia) 1,<BR> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Gaius (of Derbe) 1, Gaius (a Christian) 1; 5|of Latin origin||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Gaius = \"lord\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a Macedonian who accompanied Paul in his travelsINDENT0--<b>2)</b> a man from Derbe who went with Paul from Corinth in his last journey to JerusalemINDENT0--<b>3)</b> a man of Corinth who was his host in his second sojourn in that cityINDENT0--<b>4)</b> an unknown Christian to whom John's third epistle is addressed<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Γάϊος G&#xE1;&#xEF;os, gah&#39;-ee-os; of Latin origin; Gaius (i.e. Caius), a Christian:&#8212;Gaius.";
