Lex["1220"] = "denarion {day-nar'-ee-on}|AV - penny 9, pence 5, pennyworth 2; 16|of Latin origin||noun neuter| INDENT0--denarius = \"containing ten\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> A Roman silver coin in NT time. It took its name from it being equal to ten \"asses\", a number after 217 B.C. increased to sixteen (about 3.898 grams or .1375 oz.). It was the principal silver coin of the Roman empire. From the parable of the labourers in the vineyard, it would seem that a denarius was then the ordinary pay for a day's wages. (Mt. 20:2-13)<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> δηνάριον d&#x113;n&#xE1;rion, day-nar&#39;-ee-on; of Latin origin; a denarius (or ten asses):&#8212;pence, penny(-worth).";
Lex["1221"] = "depote {day'-pot-eh}|AV - whatsoever +3769 1; 1|from 1211 and 4218||adverb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> now at lengthINDENT0--<b>2)</b> at any timeINDENT0--<b>3)</b> at lastINDENT0--<b>4)</b> just exactly<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> δήποτε d&#x1E17;pote, day&#39;-pot-eh; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1211&page=1\">G1211</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4218&page=1\">G4218</a>; a particle of generalization; indeed, at any time:&#8212;(what-)soever.";
