Lex["1984"] = "episkope {ep-is-kop-ay'}|AV - visitation 2, bishoprick 1, office of a bishop 1; 4|from 1980|TDNT - 2:606,244|noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> investigation, inspection, visitationINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> that act by which God looks into and searches out the ways, deeds character, of men, in order to adjudge them their lot accordingly, whether joyous or sadINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> oversightINDENT7--<b>1)</b> overseership, office, charge, the office of an elderINDENT7--<b>2)</b> the overseer or presiding officers of a Christian church<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἐπισκοπή episkop&#x1E17;, ep-is-kop-ay&#39;; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1980&page=1\">G1980</a>; inspection (for relief); by implication, superintendence; specially, the Christian \"episcopate\":&#8212;the office of a \"bishop\", bishoprick, visitation.";
Lex["1985"] = "episkopos {ep-is'-kop-os}|AV - bishop 6, overseer 1; 7|from 1909 and 4649 (in the sense of 1983)|TDNT - 2:608,244|noun masculine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> an overseerINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> a man charged with the duty of seeing that things to be done by others are done rightly, any curator, guardian or superintendentINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> the superintendent, elder, or overseer of a Christian church<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἐπίσκοπος ep&#xED;skopos, ep-is&#39;-kop-os; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1909&page=1\">G1909</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4649&page=1\">G4649</a> (in the sense of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1983&page=1\">G1983</a>); a superintendent, i.e. Christian officer in genitive case charge of a (or the) church (literally or figuratively):&#8212;bishop, overseer.";
Lex["1986"] = "epispaomai {ep-ee-spah'-om-ahee}|AV - become circumcised 1; 1|from 1909 and 4685||verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> to draw on (let him not draw on his foreskin)<BR><BR> From the days of Antiochus Epiphanes [B.C. 175-164] down, there had been Jews who, in order to conceal from heathen persecutors or scoffers the external sign of their nationality, circumcision, sought artificially to compel nature to reproduce the prepuce, by extending or drawing forward with an iron instrument the remnant of it still left, so as to cover the glands.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἐπισπάομαι episp&#xE1;omai, ep-ee-spah&#39;-om-ahee; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1909&page=1\">G1909</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4685&page=1\">G4685</a>; to draw over, i.e. (with Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=203&page=1\">G203</a> implied) efface the mark of circumcision (by recovering with the foreskin):&#8212;become uncircumcised.";
