Lex["2250"] = "hemera {hay-mer'-ah}|AV - day 355, daily + 2596 15, time 3, not tr 2, misc 14; 389|from (with 5610 implied) of a derivative of hemai (to sit, akin to the base of 1476) meaning tame, i.e. gentle|TDNT - 2:943,309|noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> the day, used of the natural day, or the interval between sunrise and sunset, as distinguished from and contrasted with the nightINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> in the daytimeINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> metaph., \"the day\" is regarded as the time for abstaining from indulgence, vice, crime, because acts of the sort are perpetrated at night and in darknessINDENT0--<b>2)</b> of the civil day, or the space of twenty four hours (thus including the night)INDENT3-- <b>a)</b> Eastern usage of this term differs from our western usage. Any part of a day is counted as a whole day, hence the expression \"three days and three nights\" does not mean literally three whole days, but at least one whole day plus part of two other days.INDENT0--<b>3)</b> of the last day of this present age, the day Christ will return from heaven, raise the dead, hold the final judgment, and perfect his kingdomINDENT0--<b>4)</b> used of time in general, i.e. the days of his life.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἡμέρα h&#x113;m&#xE9;ra, hay-mer&#39;-ah; feminine (with Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=5610&page=1\">G5610</a> implied) of a derivative of &#x1F27;&#x3BC;&#x3B1;&#x3B9; h&#x113;mai (to sit; akin to the base of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1476&page=1\">G1476</a>) meaning tame, i.e. gentle; day, i.e. (literally) the time space between dawn and dark, or the whole 24 hours (but several days were usually reckoned by the Jews as inclusive of the parts of both extremes); figuratively, a period (always defined more or less clearly by the context):&#8212;age, <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><b>X</b></span></a></sup>alway, (mid-)day (by day, (-ly)), <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><b>X</b></span></a></sup>for ever, judgment, (day) time, while, years.";
Lex["2251"] = "hemeteros {hay-met'-er-os}|AV - our 8, your 1; 9|from 2349||pron|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> our, your<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἡμέτερος h&#x113;m&#xE9;teros, hay-met&#39;-er-os; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2249&page=1\">G2249</a>; our:&#8212;our, your (by a different reading).";
