Lex["2263"] = "eremos {ay'-rem-os}|AV - quiet 1; 1|perhaps by transposition from 2048 (through the idea of stillness)||adjective|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> quiet, tranquil<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἤρεμος &#x1E17;remos, ay&#39;-rem-os; perhaps by transposition from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2048&page=1\">G2048</a> (through the idea of stillness); tranquil:&#8212;quiet.";
Lex["2264"] = "Herodes {hay-ro'-dace}|AV - Herod, Antipas 27, Herod, the Great 11, Herod Agrippa 6; 44|compound of heros (a \"hero\") and 1491||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Herod = \"heroic\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> the name of a royal family that flourished among the Jews in the<BR> times of Christ and the Apostles. Herod the Great was the son of Antipater of Idumaea. Appointed king of Judaea B.C. 40 by the Roman Senate at the suggestion of Antony and with the consent of Octavian, he at length overcame the great opposition which the country made to him and took possession of the kingdom B.C. 37; and after the battle of Actium, he was confirmed by Octavian, whose favour he ever enjoyed. He was brave and skilled in war, learned and sagacious; but also extremely suspicious and cruel. Hence he destroyed the entire royal family of Hasmonaeans, put to death many of the Jews that opposed his government, and proceeded to kill even his dearly beloved wife Mariamne of the Hasmonaean line and his two sons she had borne him. By these acts of bloodshed, and especially by his love and imitation of Roman customs and institutions and by the burdensome taxes imposed upon his subjects, he so alienated the Jews that he was unable to regain their favour by his splendid restoration of the temple and other acts of munificence. He died in the 70th year of his age, the 37th year of his reign, the 4th before the Dionysian era. In his closing years John the Baptist and Christ were born; Matthew narrates that he commanded all the male children under two years old in Bethlehem to be slain.INDENT0--<b>2)</b> Herod surnamed \"Antipas\", was the son of Herod the Great and Malthace, a Samaritan woman. After the death of his father he was appointed by the Romans tetrarch of Galilee and Peraea. His first wife was the daughter of Aretas, king of Arabia; but he subsequently repudiated her and took to himself Herodias, the wife of his brother Herod Philip; and in consequence Aretas, his father-in-law, made war against him and conquered him. He cast John the Baptist into prison because John had rebuked him for this unlawful connection; and afterwards, at the instigation of Herodias, he ordered him to be beheaded. Induced by her, too, he went to Rome to obtain from the emperor the title of king. But in consequence of the accusations brought against him by Herod Agrippa I, Caligula banished him (A.D. 39) to Lugdunum in Gaul, where he seems to have died. He was light minded, sensual and vicious.INDENT0--<b>3)</b> Herod Agrippa I was the son of Aristobulus and Berenice, and grandson of Herod the Great. After various changes in fortune, he gained the favour of Caligula and Claudius to such a degree that he gradually obtained the government of all of Palestine, with the title of king. He died at Caesarea, A.D. 44, at the age of 54, in the seventh [or the 4th, reckoning from the extension of his dominions by Claudius] year of his reign, just after having ordered James the apostle, son of Zebedee, to be slain, and Peter to be cast into prison: Acts 12:21INDENT0--<b>4)</b> (Herod) Agrippa II, son of Herod Agrippa I. When his father died he was a youth of seventeen. In A.D. 48 he received from Claudius Caesar the government of Chalcis, with the right of appointing the Jewish high priests, together with the care and oversight of the temple at Jerusalem. Four years later Claudius took from him Chalcis and gave him instead a larger domain, of Batanaea, Trachonitis, and Gaulanitis, with the title of king. To those reigns Nero, in A.D. 53, added Tiberias and Taricheae and Peraean Julias, with fourteen neighbouring villages. He is mentioned in Acts 25 and 26. In the Jewish war, although he strove in vain to restrain the fury of the seditious and bellicose populace, he did not desert to the Roman side. After the fall of Jerusalem, he was<BR> vested with praetorian rank and kept the kingdom entire until his death, which took place in the third year of the emperor Trajan, [the 73rd year of his life, and the 52nd of his reign] He was the last representative of the Herodian dynasty.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>Ἡρώδης H&#x113;r&#x1E53;d&#x113;s, hay-ro&#39;-dace; compound of &#x1F25;&#x3C1;&#x3C9;&#x3C2; h&#x1E17;r&#x14D;s (a \"hero\") and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1491&page=1\">G1491</a>; heroic; Herod, the name of four Jewish kings:&#8212;Herod.";
Lex["2265"] = "Herodianoi {hay-ro-dee-an-oy'}|AV - Herodians 3; 3|plural of a derivative of 2264||noun masculine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> Herodians, i.e. Herod's partisans. See note on 2264.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>Ἡρωδιανοί H&#x113;r&#x14D;diano&#xED;, hay-ro-dee-an-oy&#39;; plural of a derivative of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2264&page=1\">G2264</a>; Herodians, i.e. partisans of Herod:&#8212;Herodians.";
Lex["2266"] = "Herodias {hay-ro-dee-as'}|AV - Herodias 6; 6|from 2264||noun proper feminine| INDENT0--Herodias = \"heroic\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> daughter of Aristobulus and granddaughter of Herod the Great. She was first married to Herod Philip I, son of Herod the Great, a man in private life; but afterwards formed an unlawful union with Herod Antipas, whom she induced not only to slay John the Baptist but also to make the journey to Rome which ruined him; at last she followed him to exile in Gaul.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>Ἡρωδιάς H&#x113;r&#x14D;di&#xE1;s, hay-ro-dee-as&#39;; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2264&page=1\">G2264</a>; Herodias, a woman of the Heodian family:&#8212;Herodias.";
Lex["2267"] = "Herodion {hay-ro-dee'-ohn}|AV - Herodian 1; 1|from 2264||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Herodian = \"heroic\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a certain Christian<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>Ἡρωδίων H&#x113;r&#x14D;d&#xED;&#x14D;n, hay-ro-dee&#39;-ohn; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2264&page=1\">G2264</a>; Herodion, a Christian:&#8212;Herodion.";
Lex["2268"] = "Hesaias {hay-sah-ee'-as}|AV - Esaias 21; 21|of Hebrew origin 03470||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Isaiah = \"Jehovah's help\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a famous Hebrew prophet who prophesied in the reigns of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Ἡσαΐας H&#x113;sa&#x1E2F;as, hay-sah-ee&#39;-as; of Hebrew origin (Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H3470\">H3470</a>); Hesaias (i.e. Jeshajah), an Israelite:&#8212;Esaias.";
Lex["2269"] = "Esau {ay-sow'}|AV - Esau 3; 3|of Hebrew origin 06215|TDNT - 2:953,311|noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Esau = \"hairy\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> was the eldest son of Isaac and twin brother of Jacob<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Ἠσαῦ &#x112;sa&#xFB;, ay-sow&#39;; of Hebrew origin (Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H6215\">H6215</a>); Esau, an Edomite:&#8212;Esau.";
Lex["2270"] = "hesuchazo {hay-soo-khad'-zo}|AV - hold (one's) peace 2, rest 1, cease 1, be quiet 1; 5|from the same as 2272||verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> to keep quietINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> to rest, cease from labourINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> to lead a quiet life, said of those who are not running hither and thither, but stay at home and mind their businessINDENT3-- <b>c)</b> to be silent, i.e. to say nothing, hold one's peace<BR> <!--For Synonyms see entry 5847<br />--><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἡσυχάζω h&#x113;sych&#xE1;z&#x14D;, hay-soo-khad&#39;-zo; from the same as Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2272&page=1\">G2272</a>; to keep still (intransitively), i.e. refrain from labor, meddlesomeness or speech:&#8212;cease, hold peace, be quiet, rest.";
Lex["2271"] = "hesuchia {hay-soo-khee'-ah}|AV - silent 3, quietness 1; 4|from 2272||noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> quietnessINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> description of the life of one who stays at home doing his own work, and does not officiously meddle with the affairs of othersINDENT0--<b>2)</b> silence<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἡσυχία h&#x113;sych&#xED;a, hay-soo-khee&#39;-ah; feminine of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2272&page=1\">G2272</a>; (as noun) stillness, i.e. desistance from bustle or language:&#8212;quietness, silence.";
Lex["2272"] = "hesuchios {hay-soo'-khee-os}|AV - quiet 1, peaceable 1; 2|a prolonged form of a compound probably of a derivative of the base of 1476 and perhaps 2192||adjective|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> quiet, tranquil<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἡσύχιος h&#x113;s&#xFD;chios, hay-soo&#39;-khee-os; a prolonged form of a compound probably of a derivative of the base of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1476&page=1\">G1476</a> and perhaps Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2192&page=1\">G2192</a>; properly, keeping one&#39;s seat (sedentary), i.e. (by implication) still (undisturbed, undisturbing):&#8212;peaceable, quiet.";
Lex["2273"] = "etoi {ay'-toy}|AV - whether 1; 1|from 2228 and 5104||particle|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> whether, either<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἤτοι &#x1E17;toi, ay&#39;-toy; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2228&page=1\">G2228</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=5104&page=1\">G5104</a>; either indeed:&#8212;whether.";
Lex["2274"] = "hettao {hayt-tah'-o}|AV - overcome 2, be inferior 1; 3|from the same as 2276||verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> to make less, inferior, to overcome<BR>INDENT3-- <b>a)</b> to be made inferiorINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> to overcome, worsted, to be conquered by one, forced to yield to oneINDENT3-- <b>c)</b> to hold a thing inferior, set below<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>ἡττάω h&#x113;tt&#xE1;&#x14D;, hayt-tah&#39;-o; from the same as Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2276&page=1\">G2276</a>; to make worse, i.e. vanquish (literally or figuratively); by implication, to rate lower:&#8212;be inferior, overcome.";
Lex["2275"] = "hettema {hayt'-tay-mah}|AV - diminishing 1, fault 1; 2|from 2274||noun neuter|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a diminution, decrease: i.e. defeatINDENT0--<b>2)</b> loss, as respects to salvation<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἥττημα h&#x1E17;tt&#x113;ma, hayt&#39;-tay-mah; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2274&page=1\">G2274</a>; a deterioration, i.e. (objectively) failure or (subjectively) loss:&#8212;diminishing, fault.";
Lex["2276"] = "hetton {hate'-ton}|AV - worse 1, less 1; 2|neuter of comparative of heka (slightly) used for that of 2556||adjective|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> inferiorINDENT0--<b>2)</b> less<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>ἥττον h&#x1E17;tton, hate&#39;-ton; neuter of comparative of &#x1F27;&#x3BA;&#x3B1; h&#x113;ka (slightly) used for that of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2556&page=1\">G2556</a>; worse (as noun); by implication, less (as adverb):&#8212;less, worse.";
Lex["2277"] = "eto {ay'-to}|AV - be 2; 2|third person singular imperative of 1510||verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> let him (it) be<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἤτω &#x1E17;t&#x14D;, ay&#39;-to; third person singular imperative of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1510&page=1\">G1510</a>; let him (or it) be:&#8212;let &#8230; be.";
Lex["2278"] = "echeo {ay-kheh'-o}|AV - roaring 1, sounding 1; 2|from 2279|TDNT - 2:954,311|verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> to soundINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> used of the roaring of the sea<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> ἠχέω &#x113;ch&#xE9;&#x14D;, ay-kheh&#39;-o; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2279&page=1\">G2279</a>; to make a loud noise, i.e. reverberate:&#8212;roar, sound.";
Lex["2279"] = "echos {ay'-khos}|AV - sound 2, fame 1; 3|of uncertain affinity||noun masculine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a sound, noiseINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> spoken of the roar of the sea wavesINDENT0--<b>2)</b> rumour, report<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>ἦχος &#x113;chos, ay&#39;-khos; of uncertain affinity; a loud or confused noise ( \"echo\"), i.e. roar; figuratively, a rumor:&#8212;fame, sound.";
Lex["2280"] = "Thaddaios {thad-dah'-yos}|AV - Thaddaeus 2; 2|of uncertain origin||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Thaddaeus = \"large hearted, courageous\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> one of the twelve apostles. From a comparison with the catalogue of apostles in Lk 6:16, and Acts 1:13, is seems that Judas, Lebbaeus and Thaddaeus were the same person, and the writer of the book of Jude.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Θαδδαῖος Thadda&#xEE;os, thad-dah&#39;-yos; of uncertain origin; Thaddæus, one of the Apostles:&#8212;Thaddæus.";
