Lex["3530"] = "Nikodemos {nik-od'-ay-mos}|AV - Nicodemus 5; 5|from 3534 and 1218||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Nicodemus = \"conqueror\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a member of the Sanhedrin who took the part of Jesus<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Νικόδημος Nik&#xF3;d&#x113;mos, nik-od&#39;-ay-mos; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3534&page=1\">G3534</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1218&page=1\">G1218</a>; victorious among his people; Nicodemus, an Israelite:&#8212;Nicodemus.";
Lex["3531"] = "Nikolaites {nik-ol-ah-ee'-tace}|AV - Nicolaitans 2; 2|from 3532||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Nicolaitans = \"destruction of people\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a sect mentioned in Rev. 2:6,15, who were charged with holding the error of Balaam, casting a stumbling block before the church of God by upholding the liberty of eating things sacrificed to idols as well as committing fornication<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Νικολαΐτης Nikola&#x1E2F;t&#x113;s, nik-ol-ah-ee&#39;-tace; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3532&page=1\">G3532</a>; a Nicolaite, i.e. adherent of Nicolaus:&#8212;Nicolaitane.";
Lex["3532"] = "Nikolaos {nik-ol'-ah-os}|AV - Nicolas 1; 1|from 3534 and 2994||noun proper masculine| INDENT0--Nicolas = \"victor of the people\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> was a proselyte of Antioch and one of the seven deacons of the church at Jerusalem<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Νικόλαος Nik&#xF3;laos, nik-ol&#39;-ah-os; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3534&page=1\">G3534</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=2994&page=1\">G2994</a>; victorious over the people; Nicolaus, a heretic:&#8212;Nicolaus.";
Lex["3533"] = "Nikopolis {nik-op'-ol-is}|AV - Nicopolis 2; 2|from 3534 and 4172||noun proper location|Nicopolis meaning \"city of victory\"<BR>INDENT0--<b>1)</b> There were many cities by this name, -- in Armenia, Pontus, Cilicia, Epirus, Thrace -- which were generally built or had there name changed, by some conqueror to commemorate a victory. The one in Titus 3:12 seems to refer to the city was built by Augustus in memory of the battle of Actium on a promontory of Epirius. The one in the subscription of Titus seems to refer to Thracian Nicopolis, founded by Trojan on the river Nestus, since he calls it the city of Macedonia.<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> Νικόπολις Nik&#xF3;polis, nik-op&#39;-ol-is; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3534&page=1\">G3534</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4172&page=1\">G4172</a>; victorious city; Nicopolis, a place in Macedonia:&#8212;Nicopolis.";
Lex["3534"] = "nikos {nee'-kos}|AV - victory 4; 4|from 3529|TDNT - 4:942,634|noun neuter|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> victoryINDENT0--<b>2)</b> to utterly vanquish<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> νῖκος n&#xEE;kos, nee&#39;-kos; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3529&page=1\">G3529</a>; a conquest (concretely), i.e. (by implication) triumph:&#8212;victory.";
