Lex["3823"] = "pale {pal'-ay}|AV - wrestle 1; 1|from pallo (to vibrate, another form for 906)|TDNT - 5:721,770|noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> wrestling (a contest between two in which each endeavours to throw the other, and which is decided when the victor is able to hold his opponent down with his hand upon his neck)INDENT3-- <b>a)</b> the term is transferred to the Christian's struggle with the power of evil<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πάλη p&#xE1;l&#x113;, pal&#39;-ay; from &#x3C0;&#x1F71;&#x3BB;&#x3BB;&#x3C9; p&#xE1;ll&#x14D; (to vibrate; another form for Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=906&page=1\">G906</a>); wrestling:&#8212;<sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><b>X</b></span></a></sup>wrestle.";
Lex["3824"] = "paliggenesia {pal-ing-ghen-es-ee'-ah}|AV - regeneration 2; 2|from 3825 and 1078|TDNT - 1:686,117|noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> new birth, reproduction, renewal, recreation, regenerationINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> hence renovation, regeneration, the production of a new life consecrated to God, a radical change of mind for the better. The word often used to denote the restoration of a thing to its pristine state, its renovation, as a renewal or restoration of life after deathINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> the renovation of the earth after the delugeINDENT3-- <b>c)</b> the renewal of the world to take place after its destruction by fire, as the Stoics taughtINDENT3-- <b>d)</b> the signal and glorious change of all things (in heaven and earth) for the better, that restoration of the primal and perfect condition of things which existed before the fall of our first parents, which the Jews looked for in connection with the advent of the Messiah, and which Christians expected in connection with the visible return of Jesus from heaven.INDENT3-- <b>e)</b> other usesINDENT7--<b>1)</b> of Cicero's restoration to rank and fortune on his recall from exileINDENT7--<b>2)</b> of the restoration of the Jewish nation after exileINDENT7--<b>3)</b> of the recovery of knowledge by recollection<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> παλιγγενεσία palingenes&#xED;a, pal-ing-ghen-es-ee&#39;-ah; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3825&page=1\">G3825</a> and Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=1078&page=1\">G1078</a>; (spiritual) rebirth (the state or the act), i.e. (figuratively) spiritual renovation; specially, Messianic restoration:&#8212;regeneration.";
Lex["3825"] = "palin {pal'-in}|AV - again 142; 142|probably from the same as 3823 (through the idea of oscillatory repetition)||adverb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> anew, againINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> renewal or repetition of the actionINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> again, anewINDENT0--<b>2)</b> again, i.e. further, moreoverINDENT0--<b>3)</b> in turn, on the other hand<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πάλιν p&#xE1;lin, pal&#39;-in; probably from the same as Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3823&page=1\">G3823</a> (through the idea of oscillatory repetition); (adverbially) anew, i.e. (of place) back, (of time) once more, or (conjunctionally) furthermore or on the other hand:&#8212;again.";
