Lex["3956"] = "pas {pas}|AV - all 748, all things 170, every 117, all men 41, whosoever 31,<BR> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;everyone 28, whole 12, all manner of 11, every man 11,<BR> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;no + 3756 9, every thing 7, any 7, whatsoever 6,<BR> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;whosoever + 3739 + 302 3, always + 1223 3, daily + 2250 2,<BR> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;any thing 2, no + 3361 2, not tr 7, misc 26; 1243|including all the forms of declension|TDNT - 5:886,795|adjective|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> individuallyINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> each, every, any, all, the whole, everyone, all things, everythingINDENT0--<b>2)</b> collectivelyINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> some of all types<BR><BR> ... \"the whole world has gone after him\" Did all the world go after Christ? \"then went all Judea, and were baptized of him in Jordan.\" Was all Judea, or all Jerusalem, baptized in Jordan? \"Ye are of God, little children\", and the whole world lieth in the wicked one\". Does the whole world there mean everybody? The words \"world\" and \"all\" are used in some seven or eight senses in Scripture, and it is very rarely the \"all\" means all persons, taken individually. The words are generally used to signify that Christ has redeemed some of all sorts -- some Jews, some Gentiles, some rich, some poor, and has not restricted His redemption to either Jew or Gentile ...<BR> C.H. Spurgeon from a sermon on Particular Redemption<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πᾶς p&#xE2;s, pas; including all the forms of declension; apparently a primary word; all, any, every, the whole:&#8212;all (manner of, means), alway(-s), any (one), <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><b>X</b></a></sup>daily, <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><b>X</b></span></a></sup>ever, every (one, way), as many as, <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><b>X</b></span></a></sup>no(-thing), X thoroughly, whatsoever, whole, whosoever.";
Lex["3957"] = "pascha {pas'-khah}|AV - Passover 28, Easter 1; 29|of Aramaic origin cf 06453|TDNT - 5:896,797|noun neuter|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> the paschal sacrifice (which was accustomed to be offered for the people's deliverance of old from Egypt)INDENT0--<b>2)</b> the paschal lamb, i.e. the lamb the Israelites were accustomed to slay and eat on the fourteenth day of the month of Nisan (the first month of their year) in memory of the day on which their fathers, preparing to depart from Egypt, were bidden by God to slay and eat a lamb, and to sprinkle their door posts with its<BR> blood, that the destroying angel, seeing the blood, might pass over their dwellings; Christ crucified is likened to the slain paschal lambINDENT0--<b>3)</b> the paschal supperINDENT0--<b>4)</b> the paschal feast, the feast of the Passover, extending from the 14th to the 20th day of the month Nisan<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πάσχα p&#xE1;scha, pas&#39;-khah; of Chaldee origin (compare Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_hebrew.html?strongs=H6453\">H6453</a>); the Passover (the meal, the day, the festival or the special sacrifices connected with it):&#8212;Easter, Passover.";
