Lex["4237"] = "prasia {pras-ee-ah'}|AV - in ranks 1, not tr. 1; 2|perhaps from prason (a leek, and so an onion-patch)||noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> a plot of ground, a garden bedINDENT0--<b>2)</b> Hebrew idiom i.e. they reclined in ranks or divisions, so that several ranks formed, as it were separate plots<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πρασιά prasi&#xE1;, pras-ee-ah&#39;; perhaps from &#x3C0;&#x3C1;&#x1F71;&#x3C3;&#x3BF;&#x3BD; pr&#xE1;son (a leek, and so an onion-patch); a garden plot, i.e. (by implication, of regular beds) a row (repeated in plural by Hebraism, to indicate an arrangement):&#8212;in ranks.";
Lex["4238"] = "prasso {pras'-so}|AV - do 28, commit 5, exact 1, require 1, deed 1, keep 1, use arts 1; 38|a root word|TDNT - 6:632,927|verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> to exercise, practise, to be busy with, carry onINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> to undertake, to doINDENT0--<b>2)</b> to accomplish, performINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> to commit, perpetrateINDENT0--<b>3)</b> to manage public affairs, transact public businessINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> to exact tribute, revenue, debtsINDENT0--<b>4)</b> to act<BR> <!--For Synonyms see entry 5871<br />--><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πράσσω pr&#xE1;ss&#x14D;, pras&#39;-so; a primary verb; to \"practise\", i.e. perform repeatedly or habitually (thus differing from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4160&page=1\">G4160</a>, which properly refers to a single act); by implication, to execute, accomplish, etc.; specially, to collect (dues), fare (personally):&#8212;commit, deeds, do, exact, keep, require, use arts.";
Lex["4239"] = "praus {prah-ooce'}|AV - meek 3; 3|apparently a primary word|TDNT - 6:645,929|adjective|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> mildness of disposition, gentleness of spirit, meekness<BR /><BR />INDENT0--<b>Additional information from the Outline of Biblical Usage:</b><br />Meekness toward God is that disposition of spirit in which we accept His dealings with us as good, and therefore without disputing or resisting. In the OT, the meek are those wholly relying on God rather than their own strength to defend them against injustice. Thus, meekness toward evil people means knowing God is permitting the injuries they inflict, that He is using them to purify His elect, and that He will deliver His elect in His time. (Isa 41:17, Luk 18:1-8) Gentleness or meekness is the opposite to self-assertiveness and self-interest. It stems from trust in God\'s goodness and control over the situation. The gentle person is not occupied with self at all. This is a work of the Holy Spirit, not of the human will. (Gal 5:23)<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πραΰς pra&#xFF;s, prah-ooce&#39;; apparently a primary word; mild, i.e. (by implication) humble:&#8212;meek. See also Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4235&page=1\">G4235</a>.";
Lex["4240"] = "prautes {prah-oo'-tace}|AV - meekness 3; 3|from 4239|TDNT - 6:645,929|noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> mildness of disposition, gentleness of spirit, meekness<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πραΰτης pra&#xFF;t&#x113;s, prah-oo&#39;-tace; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4239&page=1\">G4239</a>; mildness, i.e. (by implication) humility:&#8212;meekness.";
Lex["4241"] = "prepo {prep'-o}|AV - become 6, comely 1; 7|apparently a root word||verb|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> to stand out, to be conspicuous, to be eminentINDENT0--<b>2)</b> to be becoming, seemly, fit<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> <sup><a href=\"#strongs-legend\"><span style=\"font-size:150%\"><b>&dagger;</b></span></a></sup>πρέπω pr&#xE9;p&#x14D;, prep&#39;-o; apparently a primary verb; to tower up (be conspicuous), i.e. (by implication) to be suitable or proper (third person singular present indicative, often used impersonally, it is fit or right):&#8212;become, comely.";
Lex["4242"] = "presbeia {pres-bi'-ah}|AV - ambassage 1, message 1; 2|from 4243||noun feminine|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> age, dignity, right of the first bornINDENT0--<b>2)</b> the business normally intrusted to elders, spec. the office of an ambassador, an embassy<br /><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πρεσβεία presbe&#xED;a, pres-bi&#39;-ah; from Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4243&page=1\">G4243</a>; seniority (eldership), i.e. (by implication) an embassy (concretely, ambassadors):&#8212;ambassage, message.";
