Lex["4434"] = "ptochos {pto-khos'}|AV - poor 30, beggar 2, poor man 1, beggarly 1; 34|from ptosso {to crouch, akin to 4422 and the alternate of 4098)|TDNT - 6:885,969|adjective|INDENT0--<b>1)</b> reduced to beggary, begging, asking almsINDENT0--<b>2)</b> destitute of wealth, influence, position, honourINDENT3-- <b>a)</b> lowly, afflicted, destitute of the Christian virtues and eternal richesINDENT3-- <b>b)</b> helpless, powerless to accomplish an endINDENT3-- <b>c)</b> poor, needyINDENT0--<b>3)</b> lacking in anything<BR>INDENT3-- <b>a)</b> as respects their spiritINDENT7--<b>1)</b> destitute of wealth of learning and intellectual culture which the schools afford (men of this class most readily give themselves up to Christ's teaching and proved them selves fitted to lay hold of the heavenly treasure)<BR> <!--For Synonyms see entry 5870<br />--><BR /><hr />INDENT0--<b>Strong\'s Definition</b><br> πτωχός pt&#x14D;ch&#xF3;s, pto-khos&#39;; from &#x3C0;&#x3C4;&#x1F7D;&#x3C3;&#x3C3;&#x3C9; pt&#x1E53;ss&#x14D; (to crouch); akin to Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4422&page=1\">G4422</a> and the alternate of Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=4098&page=1\">G4098</a>); a beggar (as cringing), i.e. pauper (strictly denoting absolute or public mendicancy, although also used in a qualified or relative sense; whereas Strong\'s <a href=\"strongs_greek.html?strongs=3993&page=1\">G3993</a> properly means only straitened circumstances in private), literally (often as noun) or figuratively (distressed):&#8212;beggar(-ly), poor.";
